 ## Background
-The need for mesh extraction from 3DGS arises from the limitation of Gaussian primitives as non-editable, volumetric entities. SuGaR (Surface-aligned Gaussian Splatting) was introduced by Antoine Guedon and Vincent Lepetit in 2023 to address this by aligning Gaussians with surfaces [S1]. The Triangle Soup concept, utilized in the GaMeS model, extends this by linking Gaussians to explicit mesh faces for animation [S1]. Point cloud conversion methods have emerged as practical tools for interoperability with legacy 3D pipelines that cannot ingest Gaussian splats directly [14068-3d-gaussian-splatting-to-point-cloud.md].
+The need for mesh extraction from 3DGS arises from the limitations of the native Gaussian representation for applications requiring explicit geometry, such as physics simulation, texture baking, and integration with standard CAD or game engine pipelines. SuGaR was introduced by Guedon and Lepetit (2023) to address the lack of surface continuity in standard 3DGS by introducing surface-alignment regularization [S1]. The Triangle Soup concept, utilized in models like GaMeS, extends this by explicitly linking Gaussian parameters to mesh topology for editable animations [S1]. Point cloud conversion methods provide a simpler, post-hoc alternative for visualization and compatibility with legacy software that cannot handle Gaussian primitives [S1].
 
